WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.860
Species have been dying out for as long as there has been life.

2
00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.140
So how did a planet of microbes end up with so many kinds of living things?

3
00:00:13.233 --> 00:00:17.133
In a simple model, only two events change the count of lineages.

4
00:00:18.373 --> 00:00:20.933
A new lineage branches off: speciation.

5
00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:24.013
Or a lineage ends: extinction.

6
00:00:25.267 --> 00:00:30.447
So the count changes by births minus deaths: the net rate r is λ minus μ.

7
00:00:31.647 --> 00:00:36.667
If both rates are high and nearly equal, lineages turn over fast while the total

8
00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:41.347
creeps up. A slow total alone cannot reveal the two rates.

9
00:00:42.417 --> 00:00:45.797
Now stretch the time axis back to life's earliest traces.

10
00:00:47.137 --> 00:00:49.577
For most of that time, life was microbial.

11
00:00:51.037 --> 00:00:52.917
New kinds arrive later, as flags.

12
00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:58.530
Each flag marks an early fossil record: by this time, at the latest.

13
00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:04.170
Oxygen in the air is a band instead, a change dated from both sides.

14
00:01:05.233 --> 00:01:10.113
In the most recent stretch, five drops are called the Big Five mass extinctions.

15
00:01:11.473 --> 00:01:14.973
By the benchmark on screen, each lost most species, quickly.

16
00:01:16.333 --> 00:01:18.333
How sharply each stands out is debated.

17
00:01:19.433 --> 00:01:22.053
Take the last cliff, the end of the Cretaceous.

18
00:01:23.513 --> 00:01:24.513
What tipped the balance?

19
00:01:25.953 --> 00:01:31.013
Alvarez and colleagues found extra iridium in boundary rocks at three distant sites.

20
00:01:32.333 --> 00:01:36.093
Iridium is scarce in Earth's crust, but richer in many meteorites.

21
00:01:37.117 --> 00:01:40.737
They proposed, as a hypothesis, that an asteroid struck.

22
00:01:41.937 --> 00:01:43.977
That predicted a crater of the right age.

23
00:01:45.177 --> 00:01:48.137
A buried crater, Chicxulub, was found in Mexico.

24
00:01:49.797 --> 00:01:52.497
Dating tied impact and extinction closely in time.

25
00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:56.330
The evidence points to the impact as the trigger.

26
00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:03.210
Volcanic eruptions in India, the Deccan flood basalts, overlapped in time, and both

27
00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:04.210
causes were weighed.

28
00:02:05.283 --> 00:02:09.043
After the cliff, survivors can spread into emptied niches.

29
00:02:10.343 --> 00:02:13.143
Placental mammals diversified mainly afterward.

30
00:02:14.403 --> 00:02:16.243
But timing alone cannot show why.

31
00:02:17.267 --> 00:02:19.467
The fossil record cannot be rerun.

32
00:02:20.847 --> 00:02:28.847
Bacteria can. Rainey and Travisano grew one genotype of Pseudomonas fluorescens in

33
00:02:28.847 --> 00:02:29.847
two kinds of flask.

34
00:02:30.967 --> 00:02:35.087
One flask sits still, so the broth changes from the surface down.

35
00:02:36.367 --> 00:02:38.627
The other is shaken and stays uniform.

36
00:02:39.867 --> 00:02:41.027
Which will grow new types?

37
00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:48.567
Only the still flask.

38
00:02:49.767 --> 00:02:53.387
New heritable types arose and settled into different zones.

39
00:02:54.747 --> 00:02:57.167
One, the wrinkly spreader, mats the surface.

40
00:02:58.487 --> 00:03:00.847
The shaken flask kept only the smooth type.

41
00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:06.850
Open niches plus heritable variation gave repeatable diversification.

42
00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:11.210
These were new types of one bacterium, not new species.

43
00:03:12.433 --> 00:03:15.313
So diversity changes by births minus deaths.

44
00:03:16.773 --> 00:03:20.493
Mass extinctions are short intervals when deaths far outrun births.

45
00:03:21.713 --> 00:03:25.313
Then survivors can diversify into the space left behind.
